// A small mixed-arithmetic routine, written to EXERCISE OPCODES the rest of
// the corpus never emits.
//
// The purpose is coverage and the file says so rather than pretending to be an
// application. Every other corpus program is a real example; this one exists
// because "100% of corpus opcode instances lower" was a statement about the
// corpus and not about the instruction set, and sixteen of the sixty-six
// opcodes had no witness anywhere.
//
// WHAT IT WITNESSES, each verified by compiling and reading the emitted
// opcodes rather than by reasoning about what a construct should emit:
//
//   Byte arithmetic  -> Add, Sub, Mul, Neg   (Word operands take the Checked
//                                             forms; Byte and Float take these)
//   (Float conversion moved to `float_witness.kel` -- see below)
//   Fixed<16>        -> FixedMul, FixedDiv, WordToFixed, FixedToWord
//   external native  -> CallExternalNative   (`use external host::`)
//   2-level index    -> BoundsCheck          (a single level emits none)
//   checked `/` `%`  -> CheckedDiv, CheckedMod  (a BARE `/` emits `Div`)
//   for-in over `if` -> Len                   (an `if` EXPRESSION as the source
//                                              defeats the static-length guard)
//
// Divisors are constants so no argument vector can make this fault: the
// differential drives it with twenty-four of them.
//
// THE NATIVE BACKEND CURRENTLY REFUSES THIS FILE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
// Re-measured 2026-08-20 with `module_refusals`, which reports FOUR:
//
//   fixed_mix         UnsupportedOp("FixedDiv(16)")
//   len_witness       UnsupportedOp("Len")
//   is_struct_witness UnsupportedOp("IsStruct")
//
// `byte_mix` NO LONGER REFUSES: `Op::Add`, `Op::Sub`, `Op::Mul` and `Op::Neg`
// now lower for a matched Byte pair. `float_round_trip` MOVED OUT entirely, to
// `float_witness.kel` -- its single float constant was refusing this whole
// module and taking four unrelated opcodes' witnesses with it.
//
// TWO OF THOSE ROWS MOVED SINCE THIS FILE WAS WRITTEN, in both directions, which
// is why the set is re-measured rather than carried forward. `fixed_mix` used to
// refuse on `WordToFixed(16)`; that opcode now lowers, so the chunk gets further
// and stops on the NEXT unsupported one. And `len_witness` is new here — adding
// a witness for `Op::Len` immediately produced a fourth demonstrated refusal.
//
// A refusal names only the FIRST unsupported opcode in its chunk, so this list
// is a floor on the remaining work and not a count of it. `backend_support_census`
// exists because reading an earlier version of this list as a count reported
// three when the true figure was ten.
//
// Before this file the corpus produced ZERO backend refusals, and that figure
// was read as "everything lowers". It measured the corpus. The three refusals
// above are the first concrete, demonstrated items of remaining Workstream A
// scope, and they exist because a program finally emits the opcodes.
//
// So this file is EXPECTED to sit in the differential's exempt set under
// "the backend refuses it" until those opcodes are lowered. Do not delete it
// to restore a zero: the zero was the thing that was wrong.

use external host::tick

struct P { a: Word, b: Word }


shared data grid {
  cells: [[Word; 4]; 4],
}

// A MULTI-LEVEL indexed access. A single-level one folds its check into
// `GetDataIndexed`'s own twenty-four-bit operand and emits no `BoundsCheck`;
// only a per-level access emits the sixteen-bit form.
fn grid_at(i: Word, j: Word) -> Word {
  grid.cells[i][j]
}

fn byte_mix(a: Byte, b: Byte) -> Byte {
  let sum = a + b;
  let diff = a - b;
  let prod = diff * b;
  let neg = -prod;
  sum + neg
}

fn fixed_mix(w: Word) -> Word {
  let x = w as Fixed<16>;
  let two = 2 as Fixed<16>;
  let scaled = x * two;
  let halved = scaled / two;
  halved as Word
}

// THE CHECKED-ARITHMETIC FORM. A bare `a / b` emits `Div`; only the form with
// a `zero_divisor` arm emits `CheckedDiv`. `%` admits `zero_divisor` only, while
// `/` also admits an `overflow` arm for the `i64::MIN / -1` corner.
//
// Found by the `v0.2.3` line after eight construct attempts here failed.
//
// WHY IT WAS HARD TO FIND: I was searching for an unusual TYPE when the answer
// was an unusual FORM. I never questioned that `a / b` was the whole division
// surface, so no amount of probing operand types could have reached it.
//
// A `CHANGELOG.md` defect sits nearby but is NOT the reason, and the first
// account of it given to me was wrong in both directions. Verified here: lines
// 561 and 571 are BOTH under `## [0.2.0]` (line 497, next header 645), so it is
// one release section asserting both that the emission path "drops the
// stamped-zero-flag fallback in favour of the new opcodes" AND that "division
// and modulo continue to use the stamped-zero-flag pattern". Not staleness
// across releases -- a self-contradiction within one. And 561 precedes 571, so a
// top-down reader meets the CORRECT claim first and the wrong one last.
//
// NOT a defect, checked before anyone "fixes" it: 561 also says the construct
// does not catch division by zero. That was true for 0.2.0. `zero_divisor`
// arrives in 0.2.1 (line 461), so that sentence is correctly historical.
fn checked_ratio(a: Word, b: Word) -> Word {
  let q = a / b {
    ok(v) => v,
    zero_divisor(n) => 0,
  };
  let r = a % b {
    ok(v) => v,
    zero_divisor(n) => 0,
  };
  q + r
}

// `Op::IsStruct` — THE LAST UNWITNESSED OPCODE, and the construct is the
// `v0.2.3` line's after nine failed attempts of their own.
//
// It fires only when `named_type_name(ty) != Some(pattern_type)`, so it needs
// the pattern's type to be UNKNOWN at the test. An UNANNOTATED struct-parameter
// pattern does it; annotating the parameter folds the test out, which is the
// control their test asserts.
//
// AND IT IS A DIFFERENT CASE FROM `Op::Len`, WHICH IS WHY BOTH ARE HERE.
// Measured on this tree rather than taken from their report:
//
//   witness        verify()   module_wcmu   arena     load     run
//   Op::Len        accepts    REFUSES       REFUSED   n/a      never runs
//   Op::IsStruct   accepts    accepts       OK        LOADS    TRAPS
//
// `Op::Len`'s witness cannot be ADMITTED -- the strict iteration-bound check
// refuses it before it can load, which is the conservative-verification stance
// working as designed. `Op::IsStruct`'s witness satisfies EVERY load-time check,
// receives a memory bound, loads, and then dies at call time with
// `InvalidBytecode("Op::IsStruct on a flat struct; the type test is a
// compile-time constant")`.
//
// THAT SECOND ONE IS NOT THE SAME KIND OF FACT. `InvalidBytecode` is the class
// `verify()` exists to exclude AT LOAD TIME, so a legal program reaching it at
// RUN time is a load-time hole rather than a bad program. `src/verify.rs` is
// read-only to BOTH lines, so neither has repaired it and it is recorded for the
// operator. See the handoff.
fn is_struct_witness(p: P) -> Word {
  destructure(p)
}

// The UNANNOTATED pattern is what leaves the type unknown at the test. Writing
// `fn destructure(p: P)` here folds `Op::IsStruct` out and the witness is lost.
fn destructure(P { a, b }) -> Word {
  a + b
}

// `Op::Len` — AND THE CONSTRUCT CAME FROM THE V0.2.X LINE, not from this one.
//
// `Len` fires only when the for-in source has NO STATICALLY KNOWN LENGTH, so
// reaching it needs INFERENCE TO FAIL rather than an unusual syntax. Between the
// two lines, FOURTEEN guessed constructs failed. What worked was not a fifteenth
// guess: it was reading `static_for_in_length`'s own match arms for what they
// OMIT. They handle ArrayLiteral, Call, FieldAccess, Ident, ArrayIndex and Match,
// then fall through to `_ => None`. `Expr::If` is not among them.
//
// THE METHOD IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART. When a fallback path resists probing,
// read the guard's arm list for the omission instead of enumerating constructs
// against it.
//
// `Op::IsStruct` is the remaining unwitnessed opcode and the identical trick
// does NOT reach it: its guard reads a type from `infer_expr_type`, which has no
// `Expr::If` arm either, yet the test still folds out. Making inference fail is
// NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT there, and what suppresses it is not established.
// Nine attempts are recorded in the `v0.2.3` line's `tests/opcode_reachability.rs`
// so a tenth does not repeat them. NOT written as unreachable by either line.
//
// THE BODY DISCARDS, and that is the corpus idiom rather than laziness: local
// bindings are immutable, so accumulation across iterations is not expressible
// in an atomic-total `fn` body at all. `04_for_in.kel` says so and uses the same
// shape. A first draft here carried `let mut total` and would not have compiled.
//
// THIS FUNCTION MAKES THE WHOLE MODULE INADMISSIBLE, AND THAT IS NOT A MISTAKE.
// `verify()` accepts it, but `auto_arena_capacity_for` REFUSES it: the loop "has
// no statically extractable iteration bound". The two facts are ONE fact.
// `Op::Len` fires exactly when the for-in source has no statically known length,
// and a loop whose trip count is not statically known is exactly what the bound
// extractor refuses. So the property that makes the opcode reachable is the
// property that makes the loop unbounded.
//
// MEASURED, ruling out the obvious objection: giving both arms the SAME length
// makes the trip count two on every path and provable by inspection, and it is
// STILL refused, because neither the length guard nor the bound extractor looks
// THROUGH an `Expr::If`. Second-category conservative rejection, not first.
//
// CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS FILE: it can never be given an arena while this function
// is present, so it can never leave the differential's exempt set by lowering
// alone. `probe_len_reachability.rs` pins that, including the harness panic that
// would follow if all four refused opcodes were lowered without adding a
// bound-refusal exemption class first. Read that file before "fixing" this one.
fn len_witness(c: bool) -> Word {
  let a = [1, 2];
  let b = [3, 4];
  for x in if c { a } else { b } {
    let _d = x;
  }
  0
}

fn main(seed: Word) -> Word {
  let b = byte_mix(3 as Byte, 5 as Byte);
  let q = fixed_mix(seed);
  let g = grid_at(1, 2);
  let h = host::tick(seed);
  let c = checked_ratio(seed, 3);
  // Called so the chunk is REACHABLE, not merely present. An uncalled function
  // still compiles and still emits its opcodes, but the differential would
  // never drive it.
  let l = len_witness(true);
  let s = is_struct_witness(P { a: 1, b: 2 });
  (b as Word) + q + g + h + c + l + s
}
